# Cloudflare static-assets header rules. Copied verbatim into `out/` by the
# static export, and read as config by the Worker rather than served as a file.
#
# Rules are matched most-specific-first, and every matching rule contributes its
# headers, so the site-wide `/*` block below sets the security baseline and the
# narrower blocks add caching on top of it.


# ── Security baseline, every response ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The site previously shipped none of these. They cost nothing on a static
# export and they are the list a procurement security questionnaire checks.
#
# No Content-Security-Policy here: the site inlines a `<script>` in
# app/layout.tsx (the service-worker unregister) and Next injects inline
# bootstrap JSON, so a useful CSP needs either per-response nonces — which a
# static file host cannot generate — or a hash allowlist regenerated on every
# build. A wrong CSP breaks the page silently; that work is worth doing
# properly, not approximating here.
/*
  # 2 years, preload-eligible. The site is https-only behind Cloudflare and has
  # no http subdomain to strand.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  # Stops a browser sniffing a .txt or .json as HTML and running it.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # No framing at all — nothing here is meant to be embedded, and this is the
  # clickjacking defence for browsers that predate frame-ancestors.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  # Send the full URL same-origin, bare origin cross-origin. Keeps referrer
  # analytics working without leaking paths to third parties.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # Nothing on this site uses any of them.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()


# ── Content-hashed build output — immutable ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything under /_next/static/ carries a content hash in its filename, so a
# changed file is a changed URL and a cached copy can never be stale. These were
# being served `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which meant a returning visitor
# revalidated all thirteen chunks and the stylesheet on every single page view.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable


# ── Images and video — a week, revalidated after ──────────────────────────────
#
# These filenames are NOT content-hashed (`vision_new.webp`, `about.mp4`), so an
# immutable year would strand a replaced image in caches with no way to bust it
# short of renaming the file. A week is long enough to spare repeat visitors the
# 3.6 MB video re-fetch, short enough that swapping an asset lands on its own.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800


# ── Generated share cards ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Next emits these as extensionless files (`out/opengraph-image`,
# `out/twitter-image`). Cloudflare derives Content-Type from the file extension,
# so without these rules it serves them as application/octet-stream and the
# crawlers that check the type — LinkedIn and Slack among them — drop the image
# and fall back to a blank card.
/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/twitter-image
  Content-Type: image/png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
